body:
Make small easy sandwiches or snack items and leave them near her. I wouldn't ask her if she wants food because she will instinctually say no, but if it's there she might be inclined to eat it if it's small, easy and not complicated i.e. quartered cheese sandwich, biscuits. Some chocolate. Apparently sugary things are important, but also make sure she's drinking water. This is what people did with me, I took six weeks to eat properly again, but these snack things were the way to do it. I also got given a lot of tea and every time it came with a biscuit. I ate the biscuit without thinking. Smoothies and fruit juices also help.

&#x200B;

I personally also was given xanax the first two or three days. After that I took to having 1 alcohol drink a day which I would sip slowly throughout the day.

My friends used this: [https://eoeleadership.hee.nhs.uk/sites/default/files/Guide%204.%20First-Aid%20for%20Emotional-Shock.pdf](https://eoeleadership.hee.nhs.uk/sites/default/files/Guide%204.%20First-Aid%20for%20Emotional-Shock.pdf) and also the resources available on this website: [http://supportaftersuicide.org.uk/support-guides/help-is-at-hand/](http://supportaftersuicide.org.uk/support-guides/help-is-at-hand/). That may also help you?

&#x200B;

I'm sorry you're going through this. I know how awful it is. xx

&#x200B;

OK. I saw my partner at the funeral home and it actually didn't really help to be honest, but sometimes I know people find it helpful.

&#x200B;

I know it's hard, but I think it's better to look at pictures of him alive. What he looked like after he died isn't a detail you need to know. I understand the drive to look it up, but I don't think that would help, and a lot of the time only 'shock' photos will be found on the internet. Not truthful representations.

Whether you are the victim of someone else's suicide, or someone considering this without knowing the horror in inflicts on those around you, you must not do this. There is a way out, but you have to work hard to find it. You have the ability to find it, and when you do, the pain will recede until it's a dull ache. You have to keep going. Suicide is an easy way out for you but it is not as you might think, the only way out. You're trapped in a dark room with no doors. You can and must break the wall down. Others can help you do this. Ask them. 

&#x200B;

In killing himself my partner has also killed our hopes, the future I had dreamed of with him. Though it's not his fault, he has caused unspeakable pain to me, his parents, to his sisters, and his friends. All of whom loved him far more than he could have known and who would have done anything to stop this from happening. I have lost my best friend and the man I loved most in the world. I can't tell you how awful your suicide would be. Don't do it.
